│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ efficient_ry_cnot_block                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz block uses a Hardware Efficient Ansatz structure with 24 parameters    │
│                          │ per block (8 `RY` and 16 `RZ` gates) and a deep staircase of 7 `CZ` gates, leading to a   │
│                          │ total of 146 parameters and significant circuit depth.                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Because the dataset's feature map applies an `IsingZZ` coupling to all 28 possible qubit  │
│                          │ pairs (a fully connected graph), it already creates massive amounts of Z-basis            │
│                          │ entanglement. To leverage this, we only need a simpler, highly efficient layer of `RY`    │
│                          │ gates to rotate this Z-entanglement into population differences (similar to QAOA's        │
│                          │ mixer), followed by a shallow depth-2 ring of `CNOT` gates to provide directed,           │
│                          │ non-commuting entanglement without adding any parameters.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This proposed block reduces the parameter count per block from 24 down to 8. Over the 6   │
│                          │ block repetitions, the total parameter count drops from 146 to just 50 parameters. This   │
│                          │ nearly 3x improvement in parameter efficiency should drastically boost the combined       │
│                          │ score, reduce the training depth, and allow the Adam optimizer to converge in fewer steps │
│                          │ due to the lower-dimensional loss landscape, while maintaining or even improving the      │
│                          │ generalization accuracy.                                                                  │
